Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 04:03:01 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: PHIL/CHAT Re: What kinds of universes can you imagine?

> Does it make any sense at all to talk about some sort of Creator
> encoding a message in a fundamental arithmetic constant?  

No creator can be above the laws of arithmetic.  Maths seems to be
the discovery of *necessarily* true statements like values for e and pi
or of the form 'given X then Y follows'.  Maths is something you could,
in principle, deduce without any reference to reality and therefore is
common to all possible universes, real or imaginary.  As opposed to the
rest of science which requires empirical input.  I'm afraid the old
analytic/synthetic divide is alive and kicking, much as it upsets hard-
core Objectivists.  I've not read 'Contact' by Carl Sagan, but if he
suggested messages being left in the value of pi he was seriously
confused.

> A universe that consists entirely of one color?  A universe where time
> doesn't exist, and instead the static universe is defined by the
> solution to a minimization problem?  Where the rules change depending
> on the phase of the moon?  

These all seem quite possible.  Whether or not you regard time as
distinct from the other dimensions seems to be a matter of taste.  If
you treat spacetime as a 4 or 5 dimensional object it's quite easy to
view it as a static object.  And the principle of least action (which
you're hinting at) takes precisely this point of view in regarding
classical mechanics as the solution to the minimisation of an entity
called (surprise, surprise) action.

> -- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

Mike Price (price@price.demon.co.uk)

