Date: Wed, 13 Jan 1993 01:51:16 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: TECH: Energy sources

We have a large, diverse set of energy sources we could tap in 
the near future.  To whit:

Solar (thermal & photovoltaic), as Dani Eder mentioned
Coal, over 100 years supply at current rates (acr)
Natural gas, over 100 years acr
Oil, at least 50 years acr
Oil from shale & tar sands, at least 100 years acr for oil
Biofuels, esp. from metabolically engineered plants
Nuclear fission, U-235 & breeder cycle, 1000's of years acr 
Geothermal
etc.

The main issues are (a) captial costs of plants, (b) costs of
extracting dispersed energy sources, (c) pollution control/regulation,
and (d) temporary statist monopoly situations (eg OPEC).  Any of
the above sources could be massacred by sufficiently stupid policies.
Most of them could be made very cheap, given reasonable advancement 
in basic engineering technologies (eg materials, automation) over
the next few decades, combined with minimal statist intervention.

That's just on Earth.  In space, we have potentially the
entire solar flux from the sun.  A very cheap source of electric 
power might be had by tapping the kinetic energy of Jupiter's inner 
moons and captured asteroids; just string cable and catch the
plasma.  Kinetic energy might also be exchanged between planets
or tapped for electricity by reciprocating mass drivers, which
both launch and catch armatures.

