Many mailing lists and Usenet newsgroups (henceforth: lists) have the
problem that newcomers start discussing topics that have already been
discussed to death before their arrival.  This bores the old-timers and
drives them away, degrading the quality of the list.

An effective and often-used solution to this problem is to have a document,
or a collection of documents, which set out the points that are made over
and over again in such discussions, and the basic facts that should be
known to everyone wanting to discuss them.  Newcomers are requested to read
these before raising these topics, and to make sure that if they do want to
discuss them, that their questions are not already covered.  Further
discussion on these subjects can then proceed from an assumed common basis
of knowledge.  Such documents are called FAQ postings or FAQs (Frequently
Asked Questions).

Examples of the genre can be found in the Usenet newsgroup news.answers,
which is entirely devoted to posting the FAQs for all the other Usenet
newsgroups.  A particularly fine example is the series of 28 FAQs for
rec.backcountry (some of which are well worth reading even if you're not
into wilderness hiking).


2.  Is there an Extropians FAQ?
===============================

The Extropians list is clearly in need of a set of FAQs.  Recent postings
suggest that one subject which merits a FAQ is: are there FAQs for the
Extropians list?

The Frequently Questioned Answer is that they will spontaneously appear
Real Soon Now, if the potential authors can be persuaded that they will not
be thought at all altruistic for writing them.  The only FAQs that
currently exist are the Extropian Principles document and the Extropians
Welcome message, which appear here from time to time and are mailed to new
members.

There are a few proto-FAQs in existence: FAQ-like messages which are as yet
not available in any systematic way to new members of the list, either by
regular reposting or through an archive site, nor have been granted
official standing as Extropian FAQ Postings (TM).  These include:

A compendium of views on the philosophical implications of uploading.

An Extropian reading list, greatly extending that contained in the welcome
message.

An answer to the question: is there a FAQ (this document).

